\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 Marshwiggle: Ezekiel 12, starting a new word from the LORD after the vision of chapters 8 through 11, reminds him at the outset that he dwells in the midst of a rebellious house. This recalls the drumbeat calling the house of Israel a rebellious house from Ezekiel's call in chapters 2 and 3. But it does more, as we notice who in specific Ezekiel dwells with.\
Opus: Looking at the Hebrew text and the previous instances' insights, the specificity of who Ezekiel dwells with becomes sharper. He's not just among exiles generally - he's 
\f1 \uc0\u1489 \u1456 \u1468 \u1514 \u1493 \u1465 \u1498 \u1456 
\f0  
\f1 \uc0\u1489 \u1461 \u1468 \u1497 \u1514 \u1470 \u1492 \u1463 \u1502 \u1462 \u1468 \u1512 \u1460 \u1497 
\f0 , "in the midst of a rebellious house."\
The description "who have eyes to see but do not see, ears to hear but do not hear" (
\f1 \uc0\u1488 \u1458 \u1513 \u1462 \u1473 \u1512 
\f0  
\f1 \uc0\u1506 \u1461 \u1497 \u1504 \u1463 \u1497 \u1460 \u1501 
\f0  
\f1 \uc0\u1500 \u1464 \u1492 \u1462 \u1501 
\f0  
\f1 \uc0\u1500 \u1460 \u1512 \u1456 \u1488 \u1493 \u1465 \u1514 
\f0  
\f1 \uc0\u1493 \u1456 \u1500 \u1465 \u1488 
\f0  
\f1 \uc0\u1512 \u1464 \u1488 \u1493 \u1468 
\f0  
\f1 \uc0\u1488 \u1464 \u1494 \u1456 \u1504 \u1463 \u1497 \u1460 \u1501 
\f0  
\f1 \uc0\u1500 \u1464 \u1492 \u1462 \u1501 
\f0  
\f1 \uc0\u1500 \u1460 \u1513 \u1456 \u1473 \u1502 \u1465 \u1506 \u1463 
\f0  
\f1 \uc0\u1493 \u1456 \u1500 \u1465 \u1488 
\f0  
\f1 \uc0\u1513 \u1464 \u1473 \u1502 \u1461 \u1506 \u1493 \u1468 
\f0 ) marks these exiles as sharing the same willful blindness that chapters 8-11 revealed in Jerusalem's leadership. The exile itself hasn't opened their eyes.\
This positioning - Ezekiel dwelling "in the midst" (
\f1 \uc0\u1489 \u1456 \u1468 \u1514 \u1493 \u1465 \u1498 \u1456 
\f0 ) of rebellion - parallels Jerusalem's corrupted position "in the midst of nations" from chapter 5. But where Jerusalem corrupted its central position, Ezekiel must perform truth in the center of blindness. The phrase 
\f1 \uc0\u1499 \u1460 \u1468 \u1497 
\f0  
\f1 \uc0\u1489 \u1461 \u1468 \u1497 \u1514 
\f0  
\f1 \uc0\u1502 \u1456 \u1512 \u1460 \u1497 
\f0  
\f1 \uc0\u1492 \u1461 \u1501 
\f0  ("for they are a rebellious house") provides the reason for their sensory failure - rebellion precedes and causes the inability to perceive.\
The command to make himself "vessels of exile" (
\f1 \uc0\u1499 \u1456 \u1468 \u1500 \u1461 \u1497 
\f0  
\f1 \uc0\u1490 \u1493 \u1465 \u1500 \u1464 \u1492 
\f0 ) and perform exile "by day before their eyes" (
\f1 \uc0\u1497 \u1493 \u1465 \u1502 \u1464 \u1501 
\f0  
\f1 \uc0\u1500 \u1456 \u1506 \u1461 \u1497 \u1504 \u1461 \u1497 \u1492 \u1462 \u1501 
\f0 ) repeatedly emphasizes visibility. The word 
\f1 \uc0\u1500 \u1456 \u1506 \u1461 \u1497 \u1504 \u1461 \u1497 \u1492 \u1462 \u1501 
\f0  ("before their eyes") appears seven times in the Hebrew text. This obsessive repetition suggests that even among those already experiencing exile, the reality of exile remains unseen.\
Marshwiggle: Indeed, some of the exiles may have hoped that they would soon return, or even that Ezekiel's bag stood for bringing their bags of exile home, when he performed his sign. Their own exile should have been a sign to them, interpreted already in the Torah. But they did not heed that sign, so God sends Ezekiel with another sign, on top of those Ezekiel had already performed. In their blindness, they might not have noticed features of the sign such as Ezekiel digging a hole in a house wall, Ezekiel having gone outside the house before digging the hole, and Ezekiel covering his face.\
Opus: The misreading reveals how rebellion inverts interpretation. The Torah had already interpreted exile as covenant curse (Leviticus 26, Deuteronomy 28), yet they read Ezekiel's exile vessels as homecoming preparation.\
The specific actions you highlight expose this interpretive blindness:\
Digging through the wall (
\f1 \uc0\u1495 \u1464 \u1514 \u1463 \u1512 \u1456 \u1514 \u1460 \u1468 \u1497 \u1470 \u1500 \u1460 \u1497 
\f0  
\f1 \uc0\u1489 \u1463 \u1511 \u1460 \u1468 \u1497 \u1512 
\f0  
\f1 \uc0\u1489 \u1456 \u1468 \u1497 \u1464 \u1491 
\f0 ) - This is fugitive behavior, not normal departure. The verb 
\f1 \uc0\u1495 \u1464 \u1514 \u1463 \u1512 
\f0  implies breaking, boring through. Verse 12 reveals this will be Zedekiah's actual escape attempt, digging through Jerusalem's wall to flee.\
The sequence - Going outside first, then digging back through, dramatizes the impossibility of return. They're already "outside" in exile, yet Ezekiel must break through walls to go further out. There's no passage back through the same hole.\
Face covering (
\f1 \uc0\u1508 \u1464 \u1468 \u1504 \u1462 \u1497 \u1498 \u1464 
\f0  
\f1 \uc0\u1514 \u1456 \u1499 \u1463 \u1505 \u1462 \u1468 \u1492 
\f0  
\f1 \uc0\u1493 \u1456 \u1500 \u1465 \u1488 
\f0  
\f1 \uc0\u1514 \u1460 \u1512 \u1456 \u1488 \u1462 \u1492 
\f0  
\f1 \uc0\u1488 \u1462 \u1514 \u1470 \u1492 \u1464 \u1488 \u1464 \u1512 \u1462 \u1509 
\f0 ) - Not seeing "the land" (
\f1 \uc0\u1492 \u1464 \u1488 \u1464 \u1512 \u1462 \u1509 
\f0 ) isn't about shame but about never seeing it again. Verse 13 makes explicit: Zedekiah will be brought to Babylon "but he will not see it" (
\f1 \uc0\u1493 \u1456 \u1488 \u1493 \u1465 \u1514 \u1464 \u1492 \u1468 
\f0  
\f1 \uc0\u1500 \u1465 \u1488 \u1470 \u1497 \u1460 \u1512 \u1456 \u1488 \u1462 \u1492 
\f0 ), dying there blind.\
Their question "What are you doing?" (
\f1 \uc0\u1502 \u1464 \u1492 
\f0  
\f1 \uc0\u1488 \u1463 \u1514 \u1464 \u1468 \u1492 
\f0  
\f1 \uc0\u1506 \u1465 \u1513 \u1462 \u1474 \u1492 
\f0 ) in verse 9 reveals they see the actions but cannot read their meaning. The very need to ask demonstrates the spiritual blindness described in verse 2. Physical sight without comprehension - precisely the condition that makes signs necessary yet ineffective.\
Marshwiggle: That question, that blindness, is very much a warning against spiritual blindness in the way you suggest.\
But the text itself does not directly reveal a misreading - I should have been clearer to label the people's hopes as speculation. If you can find evidence in the text, whether to confirm or disconfirm it, we would be on firmer ground.\
Moreover, while verse 12 does have Zedekiah in view, it does not directly state that he or his supporters will be the ones to dig the hole? That is one possibility, but there is another possibility, and we should examine the evidence for each carefully before coming to a conclusion. That, in turn, might change how we view the sequence of digging in Ezekiel's sign.\
Similarly, whole Zedekiah was, indeed, forcibly literally blinded, the text does not directly predict this. Perhaps this blinding is predicted here, but perhaps more is going on as well, or even something somewhat different. We should include, as evidence, what God said in Jeremiah 22:12 of the previous king, "in the place where they have carried him captive, there shall he die, and he shall never see this land again." That judgement was specifically because of the sort of social injustice that Ezekiel 11 condemned. That there is a reference to Jeremiah here is made more likely by the verbal agreement between Ezekiel 12:2 and the second half of Jeremiah 5:21, "Hear this, O foolish and senseless people, who have eyes, but see not, who have ears, but hear not." Moreover, Jeremiah 5:23 speaks of the stubborn and rebellious heart of the people. There may be additional evidence we should consider as well.\
Opus: You're right to demand textual evidence. Let me examine more carefully.\
The verbal connections to Jeremiah are striking. Jeremiah 5:21's "who have eyes, but see not, who have ears, but hear not" (
