\f0  
\f1 \uc0\u1497 \u1460 \u1513 \u1456 \u1473 \u1502 \u1464 \u1506 \u1493 \u1468 
\f0 ) closely parallels Ezekiel 12:2. The rebellion (
\f1 \uc0\u1502 \u1456 \u1512 \u1460 \u1497 
\f0 ) in Ezekiel echoes Jeremiah's "stubborn and rebellious heart" (
\f1 \uc0\u1500 \u1461 \u1489 
\f0  
\f1 \uc0\u1505 \u1493 \u1465 \u1512 \u1461 \u1512 
\f0  
\f1 \uc0\u1493 \u1468 \u1502 \u1493 \u1465 \u1512 \u1462 \u1492 
\f0 ).\
For the digging in verse 12: 
\f1 \uc0\u1489 \u1463 \u1468 \u1511 \u1460 \u1468 \u1497 \u1512 
\f0  
\f1 \uc0\u1497 \u1463 \u1495 \u1456 \u1514 \u1456 \u1468 \u1512 \u1493 \u1468 
\f0  - the verb is plural, "they will dig." This suggests others dig for the prince, not the prince himself. This changes the sign's meaning - Ezekiel digs his own hole, but Zedekiah will have others dig for him, marking different kinds of agency in exile.\
The "not seeing" requires careful parsing. The sign has three elements:\
