\f0 ) - verse 13\uc0\u8232 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sl280\sa240\partightenfactor0
\cf2 The emphatic construction in verse 12 - 
\f1 \uc0\u1500 \u1463 \u1506 \u1463 \u1497 \u1460 \u1503 
\f0  
\f1 \uc0\u1492 \u1493 \u1468 \u1488 
\f0  ("by eye, he himself") - seems to stress personal, direct seeing. Combined with Jeremiah 22:12's precedent of never seeing the homeland again, this could primarily mean Zedekiah will never see Judah with his own eyes again, rather than explicitly predicting physical blinding.\
What additional evidence should we consider for understanding these layers of "not seeing"?\
Marshwiggle: As additional evidence with respect to the not seeing, I note that "by eye, he himself" follows his covering his face so that he cannot see.\
As for the digging in verse 12, the verb is indeed plural, so any interpretation needs multiple diggers. And Zedekiah's supporters could be those diggers. But so could be the Babylonian forces breaking through the wall. That is the other possibility I mentioned, and we should look at the evidence for each. What we say will be on a more rigorous basis having done so. Only then can we look at questions of agency.\
Opus: You're right about the sequence - the face covering comes first, then the emphatic "by eye, he himself." This ordering suggests the covering causes or relates to the not-seeing by his own eye.\
For the plural diggers, let me examine both possibilities:\
Evidence for Zedekiah's supporters:\
