\cf2 But I acknowledge the Babylonian breach remains possible. What other textual evidence might help determine which reading is stronger?\
Marshwiggle: Ezekiel is outside his house when he digs - he is to leave the house, and the digging is in their sight. The Babylonian forces are those outside.\
Moreover, in the instructions given in verse 3, and verse 4, the actions are described as going into exile. While flight is of course a possible way to become an exile, the Jeremiah Ezekiel sequence represents the exile as involuntary, with the LORD as the cause, and Babylon as the LORD's agent, as in verse 13 'I will bring him to Babylon'.\
Lastly, in the earlier embodied signs of chapters 4 and 5, Ezekiel sometimes represented the people, and sometimes represented the LORD. That should keep us from assuming that what he represents at one moment in this sign is what he represents at another moment. Clearly, in carrying a bag, he represents the people. That should incline us to think he represents the people in what follows - but only somewhat, given the chapter 4 and 5 precedent.\
But there may be more evidence for the other possibility as well.\
Opus: Your observation about Ezekiel being outside when he digs fundamentally shifts the reading. He's already exited with his vessels, then digs from the outside position - precisely where besieging forces would be.\
The involuntary exile framing throughout strengthens this. Verse 11 - "in exile, in captivity they will go" (
