\cf2 This reading would mean 
\f1 \uc0\u1497 \u1463 \u1495 \u1456 \u1514 \u1456 \u1468 \u1512 \u1493 \u1468 
\f0  ("they will dig") refers to Babylonian forces, and Zedekiah goes through the breach they create - not his own escape route but the enemy's entry point becoming his exit, inverting his agency completely.\
The face covering after representing Babylon's breach might then signify something beyond individual blindness. What additional evidence might clarify what Ezekiel represents in that final gesture?\
Marshwiggle: For that, we need to look at the answer to the people's question, what are you doing?. Ezekiel was an embodied sign, an object lesson - but he was also given an interpretation of the sign.\
Ezekiel may be continuing to follow Jeremiah, applying Jeremiah's prophesy to a new situation. Jeremiah was asked - in the chapter following 22, in 23:33 - "what is the burden of the LORD", and the LORD's burden in return was "You are the burden". In both cases, burden is metaphorical. The people's question is asking for an oracle, but the word they use for it can mean a literal physical burden. So the answer Jeremiah gives, on behalf of the LORD, plays on their word choice - they are a burden, and they will be cast off.\
The answer Ezekiel must give is similar - the prince and the house of Israel are the burden. That word may be doing double duty here, as this interpretation is in a way an oracle. But also, it is an interpretation of the literal physical burden Ezekiel had carried as an embodied sign the previous day. The king himself would become an embodied sign to the nation, being carried into exile.\
So Ezekiel covers his face, that he might not see the land. The king himself would not see the land, but would instead die in Babylon. The king being an embodied sign, the people too would die in exile, rather than returning.\
