\cf2 Verse 15's "they shall know that I am the LORD when I scatter them among the nations" confirms this collective reading. The recognition comes through experiencing what their king-as-sign prefigured - permanent exile.\
The face-covering then represents not individual judgment but the sealing of collective fate. The one who should see for them will never see home; therefore neither will they.\
Marshwiggle: There is a second statement that they will know that he is the LORD which takes this further. It is not just the hand of the LORD, capturing the prince as in a net, which will prompt their recognition. It is not just the LORD exiling the people as well. It is the LORD's hand preserving a remnant, as in Ezekiel 5 and 7 where the LORD killed many with sword and famine and pestilence - and that, that they might know he is the LORD, that they might grieve their iniquity. So too here in chapter 12. Not only do they know they committed abominations, they will declare as much.\
