"But I will leave from them men of number from sword, from famine, and from pestilence, so that they may recount all their abominations among the nations where they come."\
The purpose (
\f1 \uc0\u1500 \u1456 \u1502 \u1463 \u1506 \u1463 \u1503 
\f0 ) is explicit - survival serves confession. The remnant doesn't merely know privately; they must declare (
\f1 \uc0\u1497 \u1456 \u1505 \u1463 \u1508 \u1456 \u1468 \u1512 \u1493 \u1468 
\f0 ) their abominations among the nations. This public testimony completes the recognition formula.\
This transforms the sign-act's meaning. The burden/oracle isn't complete with exile or death but requires living witnesses who understand and can articulate what happened. The prince who cannot see becomes the sign; the remnant who can finally see become the testimony.\
The two recognition formulas - verse 15 (through scattering) and verse 16 (through declaration) - work together. Judgment creates knowledge; preservation enables confession. The abominations that were hidden in Jerusalem's chambers (chapter 8) must be declared openly among the nations.\
Marshwiggle: This sign and and its interpretation, given before the event, assist in that recognition. At the sign itself, Ezekiel was told "Perhaps they will understand, though they are a rebellious house." But when the event comes in its fullness, recognition is certain.\
