Marshwiggle: That is an example of a principle has repeatedly brought up in earlier chapters, that of the LORD bringing their ways on their heads. The violence is that of social injustice, abuse of power, and murder of their own people, as chapter 11 describes in detail. The corresponding punishment now comes upon them. But, as with verses 1-16, the purpose of this devastation is clear. The people will know that he is the LORD. The LORD will be shown to have seen their violence - though many say, as in chapter 8 and 9, that he does not see. The LORD will be shown to act, and that, in justice. And, as with verses 1-16, giving a sign and an interpretation beforehand allows the event to vindicate the LORD - and his prophet as well. The Flood and the LORD's covenant with Noah afterwards should have revealed God's character, his opposition to murder, his recourse to annihilation in the face of murder. So too the teaching that mankind was made in the image of God, and the repeated teaching on the subject of murder in the Torah. But it did not - it is experiencing the event that will make them know that he is the LORD.\
