\cf2 The prophetic sign serves as a bridge between ignored revelation and inevitable recognition. When they eat with trembling, they cannot say "we didn't know" - Ezekiel showed them. When the land lies desolate, they cannot claim God doesn't see - the prophet demonstrated divine sight through precise prediction.\
This pattern suggests that God's character revealed in scripture remains abstract until experienced in judgment. The Flood narrative was history to them; their own violence-induced desolation will be education. They will know not just that God exists, but that he is the same God who will not tolerate bloodshed indefinitely.\
Marshwiggle: In verses 21 through 28, Ezekiel is given yet another word. But this time, the subject is not a sign, but a proverb the people of Israel have. The days grow long, and every vision comes to nothing. Here we have blunt directness about the misreading of the people.\
